there's a clip of former republican  senator rick santorum making the rounds  in which he seems to either forget  or pretend not to remember that when  europeans came to what is now the united  states  there were native americans here already  there were  societies and cultures and communities  already  rick santorum is a really gross guy he's  been a cnn contributor for some time  since he left the senate  listen to what he had to say to the  young americas foundation  about the united states and its  uniqueness if you think about  this country i don't have any other  country in the world that was settled  predominantly by people who were coming  to practice their faith  they came here because they were not  allowed to practice their particular  faith in their own country  and so they came here mostly from europe  and they set up a country  that was based on judeo-christian  principles  i say judeo-chris the mosaic laws ten  commandments  and the teachings of jesus christ the  morale the morals and teachings of jesus  christ that's  that's what our founding documents are  based upon  it's in our dna you know if you think of  other countries like italy  and greece and china and turkey and  places like that  they've all sort of changed over time  i mean they've been they've been there  for sent for millennia  in many cases and their culture has sort  of evolved over time  but not us we came here  and created a blank slate we birthed a  nation  from nothing i mean there's nothing here  i mean yes we have native americans but  but candidly that that there isn't much  native american culture in american  culture  so there's a lot there and we will get  into it but what i was struck by  in watching the clip is how so much of  what these right-wingers  consider their values and principles are  based on faulty understandings of  history  where their logic is often functioning  in reverse  i'll give you an example rick santorum  says there isn't much native american  culture  in american culture he says that to  defend his claim  that there really wasn't anything here  when europeans came to what is now the  united states and  had what was here when they arrived been  more valuable  we would still see much more native  american cultural influence today  it's completely backwards reasoning  there isn't much native american culture  today  because native americans were widely  exterminated they're land taken  and we simply destroyed their culture  and overran it his reasoning is  the fact that not much of their culture  is left is  evidence that there wasn't much  interesting about it  when in fact the reason there isn't much  left is american settlers decided to get  rid of it  but it's a pretty grim insight into how  rick santorum views the people that were  here when european settlers arrived  which is  there wasn't much of value to him  based on his values which are the  judeo-christian stuff that he said  about the founding of the united states  of america the truth is there was  culture  there was infrastructure there were  communities there were customs  none of it was of any value to people  like rick santorum now if you're  interested in  learning more about this the book 1491  gives you a real sense of the americas  before european settlers landed but  there's this kind of interplay  between rick santorum's religious  beliefs serving as a justification for  what happened here  and what happened here then becomes a  justification for his religious views  it's this closed loop that can't be  broken doxastic closure  is the term i've used in the past to  describe it and then we get to the  bigger picture  message which is this is just another  example  of how these right-wingers are  incredibly staunch in their beliefs  because they think they know what those  beliefs are based on  but it turns out their beliefs are based  on misunderstandings and errors  it's you know i've talked i've jokingly  talked before about  what's the next principled stance ted  cruz will take  based on something he misheard or  misunderstood this  is a really tough cycle to break because  first you have to look at their premises  and take them down one by one  actually there were communities and  cultures and tons of things here  actually we're responsible for getting  rid of those things  and so actually the situation of the u.s  is getting to where it is today  isn't that unique and it's certainly not  particularly heroic  it has a lot of ugly in the past and it  has a ton of innovation  of course this applies to a lot of the  modern  right-wing set of beliefs taxes are bad  for the economy  and so democrats are bad for wanting  higher taxes and so the economy also  will do poorly under democrats whoa hold  on a second  actually well-calibrated and  well-balanced taxation is good for the  country  the stock market has done better under  democrats it's  a lot of work as many of you know who  have tried to convince  you know your crazy right-wing uncle at  thanksgiving it's a lot of work to get  people to change their minds on these  things and of course  this particular example for for rick  santorum also has him  it's all kind of draped in his religious  beliefs  both to justify his religious beliefs  and to glorify them and this is what  they do unless we tell people otherwise  and even when we tell people otherwise  often they keep doing it anyway  so this this is tough i've not found the  solution on this we've spoken to social  psychologists we've spoken to  um uh you know so many different people  about changing minds and when it's this  deep  it is very difficult and we're going to  see really a horrifying example of this  after the next dinner after the next  segment in the last segment of the show  a really horrible example of this from  tucker carlson unmasking so that's later  make sure you're following us on  instagram for more on this  story we are on instagram at david  pakman show  you can find me 